%U A174796 2412844770335,14479410843183,87964452906330,451313038006432,2614763283085550,15660127279092208,95372340392047316,587096245815805152,3055240364224722218,17918392533380877896,108486752257024084747,667200352642839745369,4143915035211903587976,25919174322562485631693
%N A174796 Number of admissible sequences of order j; related to 7x+1 problem.
%C A174796 From _Mike Winkler_, Sep 15 2026: (Start)
%C A174796 Put alpha = log_2(7) and m_n = floor(n*alpha). Then (1/n)*binomial(m_n-1,n-1) <= a(n) <= (1/n)*binomial(m_n,n-1). For n >= 2, equality in the lower bound holds exactly when {n*alpha} is a strict record minimum among {j*alpha}, 1 <= j <= n, and equality in the upper bound holds exactly when {n*alpha} is a strict record maximum. Equivalently, the lower and upper equality orders are the denominators of the corresponding one-sided convergents and semiconvergents to log_2(7). At a lower equality order, a(n) = binomial(m_n,n)/m_n, and at an upper equality order, a(n) = binomial(m_n+1,n)/(m_n+1). Thus the extremal values are rational Catalan numbers. The first lower equality orders are 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 31, 57, 83, 109, ... and the first upper equality orders are 1, 6, 11, 16, 21, 26, 135, 244, 353, ...In particular, a(1),...,a(6) = 1, 2, 7, 30, 143, 728 agree with A006013(0),...,A006013(5). This follows from the rational-Catalan formulas at the consecutive equality orders n=2,...,6. The coincidence breaks at n=7, where a(7)=3148 but A006013(6)=3876. (End)
%H A174796 Mike Winkler, <a href="https://doi.org/10.13140/RG.2.2.35856.78086">Admissible qx+1 Sequences, Semiconvergents, and Rational Catalan Numbers</a>, ResearchGate preprint, September 2026.
%F A174796 A sequence s(k), where k=1, 2, ..., n, is *admissible* if it satisfies s(k)=7/2 exactly j times, s(k)=1/2 exactly n-j times, s(1)*s(2)*...*s(n) < 1 but s(1)*s(2)*...*s(m) > 1 for all 1 < m < n.
%F A174796 a(1) = 1 and a(k) = Sum_{m=1..k-1} (-1)^(m-1)*binomial(floor((k-m)*(log(7)/log(2)))+m-1, m)*a(k-m) for k >= 2. - _Vladimir M. Zarubin_, Sep 25 2015
%F A174796 A finite sequence s(k), k=1,...,N, is admissible of order j if s(k)=7/2 exactly j times, s(k)=1/2 exactly N-j times, s(1)*...*s(N) < 1, and s(1)*...*s(m) > 1 for all 1 <= m < N.
%F A174796 Let alpha = log_2(7). Then a(n) = #{(s_1,...,s_{n-1}) : 1 <= s_1 < ... < s_{n-1}, s_i <= floor(i*alpha) for 1 <= i < n}. - _Mike Winkler_, Sep 15 2026
%e A174796 The unique admissible sequence of order 1 is 7/2, 1/2, 1/2.
%e A174796 The two admissible sequences of order 2 are 7/2, 7/2, 1/2, 1/2, 1/2, 1/2 and 7/2, 1/2, 7/2, 1/2, 1/2, 1/2.
%t A174796 h[n_] := Module[{L = {{1}}}, For[i = 1, i <= n, i++, K = {}; S = 0; j = 1; While[7^i >= 2^(i + j - 1), If[7^(i - 1) >= 2^(i + j - 2), S = S + L[[i, j]]]; AppendTo[K, S]; j = j + 1]; AppendTo[L, K]; ]; Return[Map[Last, Drop[L, 1]]]]
%o A174796 (PARI)
%o A174796 n=20; a=vector(n); log72=log(7)/log(2);
%o A174796 {a[1]=1; for ( k=1, n-1, a[k+1]=sum( m=1,k,(-1)^(m-1)*binomial( floor( (k-m+1)*log72)+m-1,m)*a[k-m+1] ); print1(a[k], ", ") );} \\ _Vladimir M. Zarubin_, Sep 25 2015
%Y A174796 Similar to A100982 and A174795, but for the 7x+1 problem.
%Y A174796 Cf. A100982 and A174795, the analogous admissible-sequence counts for the 3x+1 and 5x+1 problems; A028509, the continued fraction expansion of log_2(7); A006013, which agrees with a(n) for n=1,...,6.
